.TH GIN 1 "November 2026" "gin 0.1.0-dev" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
gin \- initialize local files or connect a selected Grappa window
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gin \-\-help
.br
.B gin \-\-version
.br
.B gin init
.RB [ \-f " FILE"]
.RB [ \-d " ROOT"]
.RB [ \-n " NETWORK"]
.RB [ \-c " TARGET"]
.RB [ \-T " CHANTYPES"]
.br
.B gin check
.RB [ \-f " FILE"]
.RB [ \-d " ROOT"]
.RB [ \-n " NETWORK"]
.RB [ \-c " TARGET"]
.RB [ \-T " CHANTYPES"]
.br
.B gin run
.RB [ \-f " FILE"]
.RB [ \-d " ROOT"]
.RB [ \-n " NETWORK"]
.RB [ \-c " TARGET"]
.RB [ \-T " CHANTYPES"]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gin
is an independent C99/POSIX file/FIFO frontend skeleton for Grappa's
REST resources and Phoenix Channels events. It is not an IRC client.
Local init/check/run remain unchanged. Separate connect/connect-check and
credential-import commands provide verified HTTPS/WSS startup and live selected-
window plaintext reception with FIFO SEND/JOIN/PART/QUERY. Reconnect, history
and E2EE remain deferred; this is not a beta. Live Grappa
interoperability is unqualified.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print usage and the implementation boundary.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the software version, not a supported Grappa protocol version.
.TP
.B init
Initialize one local target. All effective root/network/target settings are
required, from CLI options or an explicit file. No daemon is started.
.TP
.B check
Validate file syntax and effective settings only, without inspecting or
mutating the output filesystem or contacting a server. This is not a filesystem
or server probe: nonexistent or symlinked output roots can pass check but fail
init. Successful check prints nothing.
.TP
.B run
Initialize/reuse one window and read its local FIFO in the foreground while
holding the root writer lock. Before resource acquisition, announce startup
intent and transport unavailable on stderr, not readiness. Diagnostics may block
only before acquisition or after cleanup, with original signal dispositions;
no runtime callback writes output. A successful nonblocking FIFO writer open
establishes a local reader, not delivery.
Ignore empty LF records; fail with exit 1 on the first nonempty complete or
invalid record. No input is echoed to out or claimed accepted or delivered.
SIGINT/SIGTERM stop cleanly with exit 0 unless cleanup fails.
No fork, daemon, installed service or network connection is created.
.PP
For each command, each CLI flag may appear at most once. CLI settings override
file settings regardless of option order. Unknown options/operands are errors.
.SH CONNECTED STARTUP AND RECEPTION
.B gin connect
[-f SESSION_FILE] [-d ROOT] [-n NETWORK] [-c TARGET] [-T CHANTYPES]
[-e HTTPS_ORIGIN] [-m plain] [-a CA_FILE] [-R STATUS_FD] [-O OP_FD] [-E]
.PP
.B gin connect-check
accepts the same grammar, but validates only safe settings and effective lexical
values: no root/store/lock/CA/fd/server probe or mutation. Relative paths use cwd.
.PP
.B gin credential-import
-d ROOT -e HTTPS_ORIGIN -s PRIVATE_BEARER_FILE [-a CA_FILE]
explicitly enrolls a raw bearer from an owned0600 single-link regular file with
no final symlink. Read/close the input before acquiring root; consume exactly
1..4096 RFC6750 bytes without trimming LF/CR/NUL. Never put token bytes in argv,
environment, ordinary settings, URL or diagnostics. This command does fresh
anonymous config, authenticated account identity and explicit safe local save.
An error after rename/fsync may follow publication; do not retry or claim rollback.
.PP
ROOT selects exactly ROOT/.gin.credentials, one exact origin/account per held
root. Connect instead validates stored origin/UUID binding and never imports,
rewrites or revokes credentials implicitly. There is no arbitrary store path.
Existing local invocations never silently connect or change format.
.PP
The separate SESSION_FILE uses legacy safe0600, byte/line, no-final-symlink and
duplicate/unknown/control-byte rules. Keys are root/network/target/chantypes,
origin/mode/ca_file/connect_ms/total_ms/join_ms/startup_ms. Explicit HTTPS authority
and mode=plain are required; no userinfo/path/query/fragment or insecure fallback.
Plain is an owner assertion, not server encryption attestation or E2EE support.
Origin limit is 1024 bytes, CA path4095; existing naming bounds remain.
Timers are canonical positive decimal milliseconds: connect/total/join<=60000,
startup<=600000, effective total>=connect. Defaults1500/3000/3000/30000;
import uses10000/20000 per-request connect/total limits.
.PP
The one foreground owner does fresh config/bound-me/boot, then fresh
config/bound-me/verified same-authority WSS and correlated required subscriptions.
Connected startup and credential-import fix C=36/R=36, independently checking
C>=server minimum and server current>=R. This conservative receive subset needs
dm_with, not every feature through36 or captured current63. Native APIs remain
caller-configurable. A selected channel
requires joined remote evidence; a query additionally requires current complete
root query inventory. Missing inventory is unknown, not empty or a nick inference.
The exact-deduplicated plan contains at most four topics: root, selected network,
selected channel/peer and boot own-nick query. Own-nick transport and a payload
target alone do not authorize a peer or another output window.
.PP
Eligible plaintext records append only to the selected window through the one
held root, outside callbacks. A copy-owned arrival-ordered metadata/chat queue
is bounded to64 rows and aggregate2MiB including owned-field/row accounting;
overflow is terminal. This is separate from the native64-operation/1MiB service
quantum. Admission qualifies source JOINED state, join reference, socket epoch
and current selected route; DM routing also checks dm_with. A previously valid
selected query disappearing is terminal; later re-add does not repair it.
.PP
-R requires canonical decimal fd>=3, an already nonblocking pipe write end.
The owner duplicates without changing inherited flags and writes only READY
followed by LF after all required joins and route/plain checks. Full/broken pipe,
short/zero write or exhausted bounded EINTR attempts fails and cleans up. No -R
means no readiness announcement. Readiness also requires actual receive drain,
not merely a completed service quantum. It is not an output transaction:
earlier admitted bytes may already be appended. No blocking diagnostic stdio
runs while resources are held.
-E explicitly monitors an already nonblocking stdin pipe read end; only zero read
stops, data fails, HUP alone is not EOF. Without -E stdin is ignored, including
/dev/null. Neither option is a chat command/input path.
.PP
Before READY the selected FIFO reader and safe output are initialized, preserving
existing logs. Complete LF records within input and actual PIPE_BUF bounds
dispatch literal SEND or typed /join CHANNEL, /part CHANNEL and /query NICK.
Controls do not change the selected destination. Selected PART reports its
outcome and terminates without executing buffered following input. JOIN/PART
acceptance is pending membership; QUERY ACK is acceptance, not inventory or
delivery. A pending query blocks new FIFO admission.
.PP
-O optionally borrows a separate nonblocking pipe write fd>=3, with actual
PIPE_BUF>=192, preserving caller flags. Each payload-free LF record has grammar
OP1 id KIND STATE code, at most192 bytes including LF. IDs increase per run;
codes belong to native enums, not HTTP status. Valid operations publish PENDING
before effects and attempt at most one terminal report. Invalid input instead
reports INPUT REJECTED and ends the owner; empty input is ignored. Full/broken
receipt sinks fail without waiting or replay; terminal failure cannot undo
effects. No -O means no OP1 output; -R stays READY-only and -E stays stdin-only.
Clean FIFO writer EOF retains the reader with a checked100ms cooldown, not an
owner stop. Partial EOF input is rejected. No local echo or retry is enabled.
.PP
SIGINT/TERM stop cleanly after existing synchronous aggregate startup/send/control calls
return; later internal requests may still occur before that return. Per-request
and startup deadlines are not hard wall-clock bounds on files, resolver cleanup
or scheduling. Service waits at most50ms per quantum. No immediate abort,
heartbeat/retry, history or sustained healthy-session lifetime is
promised. Output failures are terminal and can leave a written prefix; no
rollback, replay, crash-atomic append or durable exactly-once guarantee. Existing
log bytes/inodes are preserved, not truncated. No extra discovered windows are
projected. Teardown frees session/HTTP/children before root release, restores
caller signal policy and never unlinks the persistent lock. API status0 is not
proof of reaching READY or recipient delivery; errors1/2 retain runtime/config
meanings. See docs/SESSION.md for the complete contract and limits.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-f " FILE"
Read only this explicit configuration file; no implicit discovery or config
environment variables. Relative FILE and root paths use the invocation cwd,
not the configuration file directory. No credentials are accepted.
.TP
.BI \-d " ROOT"
Absolute or relative filesystem root. Its parent directories must already
exist. Only its final component is created if missing. An existing root
must be owned by the effective user with mode exactly 0700.
Every explicit path component is opened relative to a directory descriptor
with O_NOFOLLOW; symlinks and any parent traversal component (..) are rejected.
An empty root, ASCII controls/DEL or more than 4095 bytes is invalid.
Dot components and repeated slashes are accepted.
.TP
.BI \-n " NETWORK"
A nonempty Grappa network slug, restricted in this skeleton to ASCII
letters, digits, underscore and hyphen, at most 255 bytes.
Network spelling and case are preserved, not inferred from an IRC network name.
In filenames, lowercase letters, digits, underscore and hyphen stay literal;
uppercase letters use uppercase %HH (Net becomes %4Eet). The expanded component
must also fit 255 bytes. This avoids filesystem case collisions without folding
slugs or inventing numeric network IDs.
.TP
.BI \-T " CHANTYPES"
Distinct subset of ASCII #&+!, at most four bytes; default #.
An explicitly empty value disables channels. Duplicate/foreign/control prefixes
are invalid. Overrides file chantypes. Duplicate flags/keys are errors even
when the first value is empty. File values are trimmed; CLI values are not.
Keep this setting stable when reusing a root.
.TP
.BI \-c " TARGET"
A target is a channel only if its first byte is in CHANTYPES. Other targets
are nonchannels (queries or synthetic windows, not proof of a DM).
Fold ASCII A-Z only, unconditionally for channels and nonchannels, independently
of IRC CASEMAPPING. No RFC1459 bracket folding or Unicode normalization/casefold.
Preserve original wire spelling separately from canonical identity.
.PP
Strip one configured channel prefix. A # channel has no namespace marker;
other configured prefixes use literal + followed by uppercase prefix hex:
&Foo becomes +26foo, +Foo becomes +2Bfoo, !Foo becomes +21foo.
Nonchannels use @: Grappa becomes @grappa. With default CHANTYPES,
&Foo becomes @%26foo. With empty CHANTYPES, #Foo becomes @%23foo.
Body bytes outside a-z, digits, underscore, hyphen and dot use uppercase %HH,
including literal percent, plus, @ and every non-ASCII byte.
Thus #+26foo becomes %2B26foo and cannot collide with configured &Foo.
.PP
Require strict UTF-8 scalar sequences; reject overlong, truncated, surrogate,
above-U+10FFFF and invalid continuation encodings. Reject C0/C1 controls, DEL,
ASCII whitespace, slash and empty or exact dot/dotdot bodies.
Valid non-ASCII bytes are preserved without normalization, then escaped:
#é becomes %C3%A9; decomposed #é becomes e%CC%81; #É becomes %C3%89.
Raw targets must fit 256 bytes, encoded components 255 bytes including any
namespace marker, excluding NUL. Never truncate or hash. A filesystem's smaller
limit can still fail operationally. Root lexical rules are unchanged.
.PP
The strict decoder returns the original network slug or canonical target,
not original target letter casing. It requires matching CHANTYPES and rejects
malformed/lowercase/unnecessary escapes and namespace aliases by exact
re-encoding. No old arbitrary-byte/original-casing recovery is promised.
.PP
Pre-beta layout change: uppercase network slugs have new escaped paths.
Use a new root or perform an explicit reviewed manual migration of logs.
Gin never automatically renames, deletes, merges or migrates old logs, and
has no legacy-path fallback. Back up logs and stop writers before manual work.
Common lowercase-network/default #channel and query paths remain compatible.
.SH CONFIGURATION
FILE uses case-sensitive root, network, target and chantypes keys, split at the first
= in key=value lines. Surrounding ASCII space/tab is trimmed from keys/values.
Blank lines and whole-line # comments (after space/tab) are ignored; there are
no inline comments. In particular target=#grappa retains its leading #.
LF, CRLF and an unterminated final line are accepted. Bare CR, NUL, ASCII
controls (except formatting tab/LF/CRLF), and DEL are rejected, even in comments.
.PP
Duplicate keys, unknown keys, empty root/network/target values, malformed lines and oversized
content are errors even when CLI options override every setting. Files may
provide any subset; all three effective values are required. Naming/root
lexical validation applies to effective values, using the same validator for
init and check. CHANTYPES defaults to #; root/network/target have no defaults.
There are no includes, interpolation, shell evaluation,
escape or quoting syntax. Quotes, backslashes, $ and ~ are literal bytes,
not expansion operators. Do not put passwords or tokens in this file or argv.
.PP
Bounds in bytes: whole file 16384 including comments/line endings; each line
8192 excluding LF/CRLF; trimmed root 4095, network 255, raw target 256 and
chantypes 4 (empty allowed), encoded network/window names 255.
Strict UTF-8 applies to effective targets; comments and overridden values keep
the raw-byte grammar above. No silent truncation or locale-dependent folding.
.PP
FILE must be effective-user-owned, regular, single-link, and exactly 0600
including no special mode bits. It is opened read-only with O_NOFOLLOW,
O_NONBLOCK and O_CLOEXEC, then validated by fstat before reading. Final-component
symlinks and nonregular objects are rejected; a FIFO does not block awaiting
a writer. The reader owns/closes its descriptor and handles EINTR/short reads.
Configuration parent directories are trusted and resolved normally, including
symlinks; they are not checked or repaired. Diagnostics do not echo file values,
keys or paths. No protection against concurrent changes by the same UID is claimed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ROOT/.gin.lock
Persistent regular file, exactly 0600, effective-user-owned and single-link.
init acquires a non-waiting POSIX fcntl F_SETLK exclusive whole-file advisory
lock before any network/window/FIFO/log mutation and releases it after closing
child descriptors. A conflict returns exit 1. check never probes/acquires it.
run retains this lock until all input and wake descriptors have closed.
The file's bytes are opaque and untouched, not a trusted PID record.
Never unlink, rename, truncate or replace it to recover: the kernel releases
ownership on close or process death, including SIGKILL. Valid partial layouts
are completed without deleting logs or replacing existing valid FIFO/log inodes.
.TP
.I ROOT/NETWORK/WINDOW/
Directories created with mode 0700, regardless of the caller's umask.
.TP
.I ROOT/NETWORK/WINDOW/in
A FIFO with mode 0600. Only run starts a reader; writing without one may block.
Query its PIPE_BUF and write each complete LF record in one atomic write within
that bound and the separate 4096-byte payload bound. Controls C0/DEL other than
LF reject a whole record; high bytes remain opaque. No commands or sending exist.
Observed EOF rejects partial records. The reader retains its secure descriptor
and uses a monotonic 100ms backoff to avoid HUP spin and support later writers.
.TP
.I ROOT/NETWORK/WINDOW/out
A regular file with mode 0600, opened with O_APPEND, never O_TRUNC.
Existing log bytes and inode are preserved on repeated initialization.
Initialization writes no events or placeholders. The connected receive owner
appends eligible plaintext records only for its selected window. O_APPEND is an
open flag, not a filesystem append-only attribute.
.SH SECURITY
Existing directories and endpoints must have the expected type, exact
permissions (including no special mode bits), and effective-user ownership.
Endpoints must have exactly one hard link. Unsafe entries are rejected,
not replaced, followed, chmodded or repaired. Existing output entries are
checked before opening and revalidated through the opened descriptor.
Directory descriptors anchor child access; an output FIFO never causes
initialization to wait for a reader.
.PP
This is a local-user security boundary, not a sandbox against root,
a process sharing the same effective UID, hostile filesystem semantics,
or concurrent changes by the owner. Keep parent directories trusted;
permissions on ancestor directories above ROOT are not changed or required
to be 0700. Partial safe directories or endpoints can remain after failure;
initialization is repeatable, not transactional. There is no recursive cleanup.
.PP
Locking is cooperative and intended for local filesystems implementing POSIX
record locks; there is no distributed, NFS or cross-host exclusion guarantee.
Uncooperative writers can ignore advisory locks. Locks are process-associated:
closing any descriptor for the lock inode in the owning process releases its
locks, even an independent/duplicate descriptor. Do not open the inode again
or acquire a second handle for an already-held root in that process.
Locks are not inherited across fork; handles must not be used by a child.
No protection against same-UID namespace replacement or an untrusted parent
renaming/replacing the root is claimed. There is no power-loss durability or
transactional recovery guarantee, and init leaves no writer running.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
gin init \-d ./chat \-n azzurra \-c '#grappa'
gin init \-d ./chat \-n azzurra \-c grappa
.fi
The first command creates chat/azzurra/grappa/{in,out}; the second creates
chat/azzurra/@grappa/{in,out}.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Help, version, local initialization, settings check or orderly runtime shutdown
succeeded. Not connectivity or delivery.
.TP
.B 1
Configuration I/O/unsafe file, operational filesystem/runtime failure,
invalid input record or unsupported sending.
.TP
.B 2
Malformed CLI/configuration, invalid effective settings, missing/duplicate
options, or unknown command. Validation completes before output-tree mutation.
CLI syntax is checked before the explicit file, then effective settings; an
unreadable explicit file returns 1 even if effective settings would be missing.
.SH LIMITATIONS
The installed native CLI includes connect, connect-check, and credential-import
with verified HTTPS discovery, stored-origin/account-identity checks, and
/boot plus root-first WSS subscriptions under the connected owner; native
Phoenix codec and authentication are implemented APIs. Selected-window live
plaintext projection and FIFO message/control sending are wired in;
reconnection, history and end-to-end encryption remain deferred.
.B gin run
remains local-only with unsupported sending.
.PP
run uses POSIX poll and an async-signal-safe self-pipe for SIGINT/SIGTERM.
The runtime closes input/wake descriptors before releasing the root lock and
restores previous signal dispositions and mask. Connected receive uses explicit
mode=plain as an owner assertion, not server encryption attestation or complete
ciphertext detection; there is no plaintext fallback. Automatic
reconnection and history recovery are not wired into that command. Connected
policy declares client protocol36 and requires server protocol36, including
dm_with; this is not a claim of all upstream features. No live Grappa revision
or recipient delivery is qualified.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mkfifo (1),
.BR tail (1),
.BR openat (2)
